A||||10||BAM-BAM||Got a problem...need some advice!!!||||9||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||12-23-2001||02:02 PM|||| Here's the scenario; Coming home from work the other day I noticed that when I applied the brakes, there was a audible clunk coming from the left front wheel. After arriving home I had my son watch the front tire to see if he could notice any movement (noise was associated with a slight pull). He said that the whole wheel and tire assembly shifted vertically inline with the car about 1/2-3/4 of an inch. I didn't have time to mess with it with my schedule and deferred until today. I have been driving his 87GT since. <BR>  Today, I lifted the car, removed the tire and looked and shook anything and everything. I couldn't get it to duplicate. So I put the tire back on for better leverage and the only movement I could get was from the steering rack. The steering wheel also moved in conjunction with the wheel. I eliminated that as a cause. I then lowered the car and pulled slightly forward and applied the brakes and the clunk was there. I reversed slightly and the clunk was there. I had my son duplicate the same motion while I looked right at the control arm and upper ball joint. It appears that the upper control arm is shifting inline with the vehicle.<BR>  Now my question. Is the control arm suppose to shift? I assume it would a little. Could a bad upper ball joint exaggerate that movement? I would think that if the ball joint was toast, it would make it appear that the whole assembly was shifting as the ball moved around. <BR>  If anyone can be of any help I would appreciate it. I did notice that the ball joint boot was torn and that there was grease everywhere. Probably pretty academic, but I need to be a little more positive.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS- The car is an 85GT, lowered with Poly bushings and 1.5 year old Moog ball joints.<P>PEACE!!!||||reg||9
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-23-2001||02:52 PM||||Bam-Bam,<P>it sounds like something came loose.  Since the poly bushings and balljoints are fairly new you can rule them out as culprits.  I would remove the wheel again and tighten up the control arm bolts and see what happens.<P>I went through a similar thing on my '88 GT a few weeks ago.  Everytime I accelerated or lifted off of the throttle teh entire car swayed (very unsettling at 70MPH!).  i found out that the big bolt that runs through the rear spindle and the two small links (this is an '88) had backed itself almost completely out.  A few turns with a wrench and all was fine.<P>Good luck and let us know what you find out.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||03:24 PM|||| Check the suspension good like was suggested.<P> Also check the pivot point in the crossmember for the upper control arm. I have seen several break the the pivot ( piece of pipe) clear from the cross member.<BR> a few of them looked like metal fatigue, no rust.<P> The cars exhibited the same problems as you spoke of, and it's hard as hell to replicate the movement with the wheel off, and the weight off of the suspension. You have to get in there and look, and do some prying with a pry bar.<P> The control arm shouldn't pull clear from the car, as there is another bracket back there wrapped around the pivot.<BR> Do have this fixed right away if it is the problem, as it is not safe this way.<P> Fixed meaning replace the front cross member, or having it welded as your 2nd and last option.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||12-23-2001||04:12 PM||||Jelly, It's funny you should mention that cross member. I was watching as the wheel turned and it looked like the control arm bracket was moving along with the control arnm itself. I looked in my Chitons manual and it only has a vauge pictorial of the unit. Would I have to replace the whole front cross member if the C-arm bracket its broken?? I don't know that I would trust a weld. Also, how big of a PIA is replacing the cross member?? I have to get this done ASAP, as my son is going back to school in about a week and a half.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||04:48 PM||||Your in NV so you won't have to deal with seized bolts, that's a bonus.hehe<P> Here's my recommendation on replacing the cross member.<P>Raise the car, and support it on jack stands on the frame rails under the floor pan of the car.<P> remove the calipers and hang them out of the way, remove the sway bar end links, cross member supports ( both the bars running forward, and the caps on each end of the crossmember above the upper control arms. Take the pinch bolt out of the steering shaft under the dash. remove the lower shock bolts at the lower control arms.<P> You might want to remove the coil springs before you drop the cross member assembly from the car. do this by supporting the control arms with a jack, separate the lower ball joint from the steering knuckle, then lower the control arm with the jack. You will have to pry the bottom of the spring over the spring seat of the control arm then.<P> Remove the tierod ends, and the upper ball joints from the steering knuckle, and remove that. Remove the lower control arms. You can remove the steering rack now too if you want.<P> You can remove all these off the assembly after you remove it from the car if you want. Removing them first just gives you something more stable to work with.<P> After you get removed what you want, all that is left is the cross member itself. There is 2 bolts on each side that go up into the frame. Use the jack under the center of the cross member to support it as you remove the bolts, and lower it with that.<P> You can remove the upper control arms after the cross member is out.<P> Installation is reverse. Leave the bolts on the control arms loose untill almost last.<P> I like to set the car down with jack stands under the steering knuckle, or rotors to settle the suspension, and leave the bushings in the relaxed position before I tighten them control arm bolts.<P> Only hard part is getting the springs back in. I'd recommend after the cross member is bolted in to install the lower control arms, and let them hang straight down. Install the springs over the bumpstops in the control arm, then slide them over the edge of the spring seat on the lower control arm. Then jack the lower control arm up to compress the spring. Then install the steering knuckle, hooking up the upper and lower control arms. This will keep the spring in place.<P> Lemme know if you need any help along the way, i may have forgotten something, but I don't think so.<P> good luck<P> Woops, I did forget something. On the cross member, there is a bracket on the rear for the RR brake line that needs to be removed. There is also a bolt on either side that hold the coolant tubes to the crossmember. You might want to remove the steering rack first to get at the one on the drivers side.<BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">    <P>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-23-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-23-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||12-24-2001||12:39 PM||||  Thanks J elly man!! I am going to investigate this in more detail later today or perhaps on Thursday. If I need more assistance I know where to go. This is precisely why I love this Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000006||BAM-BAM||12-26-2001||08:15 PM||||  Ok I checked it out and found that the left upper control arm bracket has a lot of play in it. Does this mean that I need to replace the entire cross member? I am kind of leary about re welding it. Does anyone thing that welding it would be dangerous??? I need some quick advice.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodney||12-26-2001||08:52 PM||||big bolt that runs through the rear spindle and the two small links (this is an '88) had backed itself almost completely out. A few turns with a wrench and all was fine.<P>********************************************<P>I had heard of something like this happening to someone years ago. So when I did the change over to the poly on my suspension on my 88 I double nutted all the bolts and also used locktite to be sure.  : )||||reg||1
Z||000008||BAM-BAM||12-27-2001||01:28 AM||||  Ok, I got it out and sure enough, the upper control arm bracket was broke loose. Tomorrow I will see if a local machine shop will weld it. If not the it's off to the wrecking yard for a new used cross member. At least now I have some resolution to this situation. BTW, It was as easy as just doing it. Took a little patience at times and in the end it was those damn, coolant tube brackets that slowed me down. All together it took me about three solid hours of work to get it all apart. next time (tomorrow?) it will be much faster, much faster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000009||BAM-BAM||12-30-2001||01:04 AM|||| Mission complete. Took me all of about seven hours to do the transplant. That doesn't include the driving time to and from the wrecking yard. All went pretty much as J-elly man said, real straight forward. I ran into an anomaly with the poly's though and wound up calling Suspension Restoration for advice on what type of grease to use in place of their Super Grease. They recommended using Marine Axle Grease, and it worked awesome (even better than the Super Grease) Nice and quiet <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>  I just want to thank everyone for all the assistance and advice and send out a special thanks to J-elly Man. You made a tough situation a breeze to deal with. You Da Man!!!<P><BR>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||12
Z||000010||jelly2m8||12-30-2001||02:02 AM||||No problem at all BAM-BAM, glad I could help!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
